---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import Panel from '../components/Panel.astro';
import portrait from '../assets/daniel-nowak.png';
import { DISCLOSURE } from '../data/site';
---
<Site
  title="About Daniel Nowak | DNA4Digital"
  description="Eighteen years of operations across hospitality, BPO and SaaS. #1 EMEA revenue team at Google Customer Solutions, RevOps at Sensay, client operations at SpotOn, Associate Partner at Yameo."
>
  <Panel rung={1} kicker="02 · About">
    <h1>The bottleneck is almost never the tool. It's the handoff.</h1>
    <Image src={portrait} alt="Daniel Nowak at his desk" width={300} class="photo" loading="eager" />
    <p class="lead">I'm Daniel Nowak. I fix how revenue and operations actually run, and then apply AI where it compounds.</p>
    <p>I didn't arrive at AI from engineering. I arrived from operations: eighteen years of them, thirteen spent running restaurants, conference floors and hotel F&amp;B departments in Poland and Ireland. In that world a broken process isn't a slide in a deck. It's a room full of people waiting for their food, and you find out within the hour.</p>
    <p>That taught me the thing I still sell today: the bottleneck is almost never the tool. I do that work independently through DNA4Digital, and as Associate Partner at Yameo (a Dutch-Polish enterprise software house) when a client needs the system built as well as designed.</p>
    <div class="stats">
      <div><b>#1</b><span>revenue team in EMEA, Google CS</span></div>
      <div><b>5&times;</b><span>funnel engagement, Sensay</span></div>
      <div><b>30%</b><span>less ticket handling time, SpotOn</span></div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="mission">Why I do this</h2>
    <p>I moved into tech in 2021, and I kept noticing the same thing from the inside that I'd seen from the kitchen pass: companies buying tools to solve problems that were never tool problems. Technology without business context doesn't fix anything. It just relocates the mess and makes it faster. So I work the other way round: process first, then automation, then AI.</p>

    <h2 id="story">My story</h2>
    <p>Thirteen years in hospitality operations: opening a café and bakery in Donegal National Park, running four F&amp;B outlets and 600 covers a day at the Royal Marine, and rebuilding a hotel restaurant into a takeaway business in the first weeks of COVID with nothing but Facebook, Google Ads and a redrawn menu. Along the way I learned SOPs, forecasting, margin control, and what happens to a team when the process is unclear.</p>
    <p>In 2021 I joined TTEC as a Google Ads account manager. I was promoted to POD Lead in four months, then to Team Leader running Google Customer Solutions for Poland and CZ/SK as a TTEC vendor partner. That team finished <strong>#1 for revenue in EMEA</strong>, and I took the Platinum Club award for best manager in the region. I also held a 5/5 employee satisfaction score for four straight quarters. Nobody left, and that protected the margin.</p>
    <p>Then I went technical. At Sensay, an AI SaaS company, I stopped selling and started building: migrating legacy systems into HubSpot, enriching the data with Breeze Intelligence, engineering automated sequences in n8n and Zapier, and assembling Looker Studio dashboards that merged CRM, product and website analytics. <strong>Funnel engagement went up fivefold.</strong></p>
    <p>At SpotOn I ran client operations for a restaurant-tech platform, and the same pattern held: audit the workflows, automate the right ones, <strong>cut ticket handling time by 30%</strong>, and rebuild the SOPs so the answers live somewhere other than in people's heads. Given where I started, it felt like the circle closing.</p>
    <p>I'm now Associate Partner at Yameo, a Dutch&ndash;Polish software house that has been building enterprise systems for banking, insurance and healthcare since 2006. That partnership matters for one practical reason: when the work I've mapped needs engineers to build it, I have a team behind me that ships on fixed price, fixed date and a signed SLA. Most independent consultants have to hand you a plan and wish you luck.</p>

    <div class="pcards three">
      <div class="pcard"><h3>Why work with me</h3><p>No &ldquo;AI magic&rdquo;, and no pretending the tool is the answer. You get a transparent plan, fast pilots and effects you can measure. You also get me, not a pitch team followed by a delivery team you've never met.</p></div>
      <div class="pcard"><h3>What I stand for</h3><p><strong>Simplicity:</strong> if a process can't be explained on one page, it won't be followed. <strong>Accountability:</strong> I name the number, the baseline and the timeframe. <strong>Partnership:</strong> I only teach what I've run myself.</p></div>
      <div class="pcard"><h3>The arc</h3><p>Hospitality taught me operations. Google taught me scale. Sensay taught me to build the thing instead of just specifying it. Yameo means I can now build the ones that are too big for one person.</p></div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="approach">How I work</h2>
    <p>From diagnosis to rollout and scale, without chaos and without burning out your team.</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><h3>Audit &amp; process map</h3><p>I map how work actually moves, not how the org chart says it does. Bottlenecks, handoffs, duplicated fields, the workarounds everyone has quietly built. You get the friction ranked by what it costs you.</p><a href="/services/#audit">More about audits &rarr;</a></li>
      <li><h3>Pilot &amp; training</h3><p>We test on live examples with your data, I train the people who'll run it, and we pick tools that fit the process rather than the other way round.</p><a href="/services/#training">More about training &rarr;</a></li>
      <li><h3>Implementation &amp; scale</h3><p>Standards, SOPs, KPIs and dashboards, so the improvement survives after I've gone.</p><a href="/services/#consulting">More about consulting &rarr;</a></li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="practice">Three things I'm actually good at</h2>
    <div class="pcards three">
      <div class="pcard"><h3>Go-to-market &amp; RevOps</h3><p>Funnel and pipeline design, qualification rules, handoffs, CRM architecture and reporting. Intent-driven prospecting off ICP and persona mapping, A/B-tested outbound cadences that lifted engagement 18%, and a legacy stack migrated into one HubSpot instance that leadership could actually read.</p></div>
      <div class="pcard"><h3>Process architecture</h3><p>Mapping, standardisation, ownership, SOPs, KPIs and quality control. This is the Lean Six Sigma half: unglamorous, and the half that decides whether anything else works. Most &ldquo;AI problems&rdquo; turn out to be undocumented processes wearing a costume.</p></div>
      <div class="pcard"><h3>AI enablement &amp; automation</h3><p>Low-code architecture in n8n and Zapier, AI-enhanced training, internal troubleshooting chatbots, sales assistants and concierge bots. Plus the governance nobody wants to write: SOPs, GDPR data-sharing agreements and defensible analytics integrations.</p></div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="track-record">Where this experience comes from</h2>
    <p>Eighteen years of operations across hospitality, BPO and SaaS, in Poland, Ireland and across EMEA.</p>
    <ol class="timeline">
      <li><b>Yameo, Associate Partner</b><span>Enterprise software delivery for banking, insurance, healthcare and the public sector.</span></li>
      <li><b>Google Customer Solutions, via TTEC</b><span>Led the PL and CZ/SK team as a TTEC vendor partner. #1 revenue team in EMEA; Platinum Club, Best Manager, Q3 2024.</span></li>
      <li><b>TTEC, Team Leader</b><span>EMEA business development. Enterprise BPO solutioning and C-level negotiation.</span></li>
      <li><b>Sensay, Senior Technical BDM</b><span>RevOps architecture and AI product deployment.</span></li>
      <li><b>SpotOn, Client Operations Manager</b><span>Workflow automation and operational enablement.</span></li>
      <li><b>Balkans Crypto, Sales Director</b><span>Commercial strategy for a 2,000-attendee Web3 conference; Tier-1 exchange and L1/L2 partnerships.</span></li>
      <li><b>Hospitality operations, 13 years</b><span>Restaurants, conference floors and hotel F&amp;B in Poland and Ireland.</span></li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>Credentials:</strong> Lean Six Sigma Black Belt &middot; Machine Learning with Python (V2) &middot; Web Development &middot; BA Economics (Wyższa Szkoła Finansów i Informatyki, 2026)</p>
    <p class="disclosure" id="disclosure">{DISCLOSURE} I'll tell you that in the first conversation, not the fifth.</p>

    <h2>Ready to give technology some meaning?</h2>
    <p>Book a free 30-minute call. I'll tell you where I'd start, and whether the first move is an audit, a pilot, or simply writing down how the work is supposed to happen.</p>
    <div class="btnrow">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact/?topic=call">Book a consultation</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/services/">See services</a>
    </div>
  </Panel>
</Site>
